To solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of math and reading problems Katie has to complete. She has two types of homework: 7 pages of math homework and 3 pages of reading homework. Each page contains 9 problems. 

---

### Step 1: Calculate Math Problems
Katie has 7 pages of math homework. Since each page has 9 problems:

$$
7 \times 9 = 63 \text{ math problems}
$$

---

### Step 2: Calculate Reading Problems
Katie also has 3 pages of reading homework. Again, each page has 9 problems:

$$
3 \times 9 = 27 \text{ reading problems}
$$

---

### Step 3: Add the Total Problems
Now, sum up the math and reading problems:

$$
63 + 27 = 90
$$

Alternatively, since there are 7 math pages and 3 reading pages, that makes:

$$
7 + 3 = 10 \text{ pages}
$$

Then:

$$
10 \times 9 = 90 \text{ total problems}
$$

---

### Final Answer
$$
\boxed{90}
$$

Katie has to complete a total of **90** problems. ✅

